WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.690
- [Claire] We always say this, I know,

00:00:01.690 --> 00:00:03.760
but these are actually the best brownies

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.060
you're ever going to have.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.740
They're gooey, they're
chocolatey, they're fudgy.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.490
Oh, they live up to every expectation

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:11.990
you think a brownie should be.

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:14.616
(lively music)

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.930
We took all the tips and the
tricks from the internet,

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:20.480
from our old gramma's recipes,

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:21.620
from places we'd worked,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.210
to find the best recipe that we could.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.980
Throw out every other recipe
that you have for brownies.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.280
This is the one you will use going forward

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:30.441
for the rest of your life.

00:00:30.441 --> 00:00:31.274
(lively music)

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:32.840
I'm not trying to toot our own horn,

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.173
but beep beep, you know.

00:00:34.357 --> 00:00:36.099
(lively music)

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:38.320
All right, guys, as always, prep your tin.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.420
You know what to do, butter and parchment.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.380
So the first cooking job
that I ever had was working

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:46.523
for a pastry chef and he made
these incredible brownies.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:48.100
When you start working in kitchens,

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.920
they always say write down the recipes

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.140
'cause you're gonna forget them later.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.690
And of course, I did not do that,

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:54.310
but there are a few things that I remember

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:55.940
from his recipe that I really wanted

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.890
to incorporate into our brownie recipe.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.360
Obviously, good brownies
need good chocolate,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.460
and the chocolate bar you
wanna use is dealer's choice.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.280
You can go for something really mellow

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.590
like a milk chocolate bar
or go into something really,

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.660
really dark, like an 80% dark chocolate

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.210
would work really well in here.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:12.160
We're gonna double up on
the chocolate so we're gonna

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:13.660
use cocoa powder as well.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.633
Because we wanted a really
rich, dark flavor and color,

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.443
we went with the dutch processed.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.020
You can use regular cocoa powder here,

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.330
but we're always looking
for that intense flavor,

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:23.403
that intense color.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.850
The one on the left used
regular cocoa powder.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.470
It's kind of drier on
top and almost too dense

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:29.570
and fudgy on the bottom.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.190
We're adding a little
bit of cocoa powder here.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:32.983
We're gonna add even more later.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:35.150
Espresso powder just
does a really great job

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.763
of enhancing any chocolate
flavor in a recipe.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.090
If you're worried about the caffeine,

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:41.853
you can get a decaf powder as well.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:43.950
It's not gonna taste
like coffee or espresso,

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:46.000
it's really just another flavor enhancer.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.290
When we add the hot, melted
butter to the chocolate,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.680
cocoa powder and espresso powder,

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.890
it's gonna help melt
everything down and dissolve,

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:54.220
which is gonna help keep our brownies

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:55.523
really fudgy later on.

00:01:55.851 --> 00:01:58.434
(lively music)

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.170
Sugars, we have granulated
sugar, and then when deciding

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:03.840
between light brown and dark brown,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.470
the real difference
here is that dark brown

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.743
has more molasses, ergo more flavor.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.653
I don't even know what ergo means.

00:02:10.506 --> 00:02:13.430
Again, just wanted to knock your socks off

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:15.510
with the flavor here and dark brown

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.010
was the right one for the job.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:19.800
And we're also gonna add a bit of salt

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.100
as it brings out the
flavor in any baked good.

00:02:22.248 --> 00:02:24.831
(lively music)

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.270
So we're gonna have six eggs.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:27.860
Always crack your eggs
into a separate bowl

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:29.363
in case you get any shell.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.993
Best way to remedy that
is to use the egg shell.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.320
It breaks the surface tension
and is the easiest way

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.123
to get eggshell out of your eggs.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.130
You can start by adding
one egg to the sugar

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:41.380
just so you don't make a huge mess.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:43.560
Once you get it going though,
you can add the rest of 'em,

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:44.393
no big deal.

00:02:44.393 --> 00:02:45.520
You don't have to be super
careful like you would

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:46.843
for a cake for instance.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:48.460
So working for that pastry chef,

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.650
one thing that he did with
his brownies is he would beat

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:54.010
the living crap out of the
eggs and sugar and really,

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:55.640
really incorporate tons of air,

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:56.880
so much so that it would look like

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.943
a super thick pancake batter almost.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:02.410
This is what happens when you
don't beat the sugar and eggs,

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.363
kind of just falls really flat, right.

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:06.840
The great thing about beating
the eggs and the sugar

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.510
is you create a really solid foundation

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.453
and you don't have to
use a chemical leavener.

00:03:12.285 --> 00:03:15.610
Then you're gonna pour
in that beautiful ganache

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:16.463
that we made.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.213
Oh my God.

00:03:19.991 --> 00:03:21.060
Actually, when we were shooting this,

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.720
there is a crowd of
people around us 'cause

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:27.233
we couldn't get over how
insanely delicious this looked.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.693
Oh God, I just, I can't
believed we baked these.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:32.230
Honestly, I could've just
eaten the batter myself.

00:03:32.261 --> 00:03:34.700
(lively music)

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.180
I'm really upset that America can't get

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.980
on the metric system, but like fine.

00:03:38.573 --> 00:03:41.010
If we're gonna use cups,
always scoop and level,

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.800
to make sure we have the
right amount of flour.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.950
If you just take the cup
straight into the flour,

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:46.970
it's gonna be denser
than you need it to be.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:49.190
And you're going to sift the
flour and the cocoa powder

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:51.140
into the mixture to make
sure we have no lumps

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:52.460
and just make sure that we're really

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.110
quickly incorporating it
into the rest of the batter.

00:03:55.123 --> 00:03:57.706
(lively music)

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.453
And fold.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.760
Because we beat all that
air in with the eggs,

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.070
we don't wanna totally deflate that,

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:06.130
so just get the dry
ingredients incorporated

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:07.230
as quickly as you can.

00:04:07.452 --> 00:04:09.045
(lively music)

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:12.270
Pour the completed batter
into your prepped tin,

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.823
smooth it out to make sure
everything's pretty level.

00:04:14.897 --> 00:04:17.430
(lively music)

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:18.870
And into the oven it goes.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:20.693
So these are gonna rise quite a bit.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.440
After about 20 minutes,
we're gonna take them out.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.300
This is my favorite tip
of the whole recipe.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.733
Take the brownies out and slam
them on your kitchen counter.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:30.910
It's gonna crack the top as
well as evens out everything

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:33.610
and you get a much more
consistent texture.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:34.690
These are the same recipe.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:37.483
The one on the left is not
whacked, right whacked.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.370
At this point, we're also
going to add a bit of sea salt,

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:42.050
optional but highly recommended.

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:45.363
Adds a little salty bite,
sweet and salty, always good.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.810
With a lot of baked goods,
you'll stick a toothpick in

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.640
and no batter remains, then you're good.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.270
It's kind of not the
case with these brownies.

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.650
You will have a good amount
of fudginess that comes

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:54.900
out on the toothpick.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.060
Trust us, they've been in
there for like 45 minutes.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:58.370
They're definitely cooked through.

00:04:58.370 --> 00:04:59.763
They're just fudgy.

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:03.280
When they do cool down, they
also will deflate quite a bit.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.920
It results in a really even
texture all throughout.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:07.520
Again, I can't say fudgy enough.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.090
I don't even like fudge,
little known thing about me,

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:11.590
but these brownies are insane.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:12.970
I need to cut these nice.

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:14.670
They need to be a good, perfect square.

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.440
It's so satisfying to see a perfectly cut

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:18.890
batch of brownies, obviously.

00:05:19.282 --> 00:05:20.190
(lively music)

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:22.330
Oh my gosh, a little tip too,

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:24.110
is to clean your knife after each cut.

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:26.600
You'll see a little bit of
that fudginess comes off.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:28.400
They're fudgy but they're not dense.

00:05:28.465 --> 00:05:31.048
(lively music)

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:33.530
And that is it.

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:35.723
I mean, look at that texture.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:36.890
Look at it!

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:38.740
This is like your go to brownie batter.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:39.790
It's amazing as is,

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:41.610
but you can also mix in
anything that you want,

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:44.418
more chocolate chunks,
potato chips, pretzels.

00:05:44.418 --> 00:05:47.260
This is like a phenomenal
standard brownie recipe

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.943
but feel free to go crazy
and mix in whatever you want.

00:05:49.943 --> 00:05:51.900
Making brownies from scratch, you know,

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:53.770
there's other options out
there that are easier,

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:55.880
but it's one of those
fundamental baked goods

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:58.033
that doesn't take a ton of technique,

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.500
but they also go with
everything and every event.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:01.333
You know what I mean?

00:06:01.333 --> 00:06:02.960
Like you have a break up, brownies.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.253
Birthday, brownies.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:06.733
Like, they kind of work for anything.

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:10.260
Just one of those feel good,
crowd pleasing deserts,

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:11.890
that's just always a win.

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:13.200
Okay, that's fine.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.540
I'm just gonna keep
talking and we're leave it

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.573
and it's gonna be fine.

00:06:15.573 --> 00:06:17.162
- [Male] You're funny, Claire.

00:06:17.162 --> 00:06:18.134
- [Claire] (laughing) Stop.

00:06:18.134 --> 00:06:18.967
- [Male] You're so funny.

00:06:18.967 --> 00:06:19.800
- [Claire] You're so funny.

00:06:20.127 --> 00:06:20.960
- [Male] You're funny.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:21.793
- [Claire] Beep, beep.

00:06:21.793 --> 00:06:22.626
- [Male] You're slacking.

00:06:22.626 --> 00:06:23.937
- [Claire] I guess.

00:06:23.937 --> 00:06:26.770
(camera clicking)

